text:
A part of sexual pleasure is evolutionary, encouraging males to reproduce. This involves a series of pleasurable sensations leading up to and following orgasm, all of which make copulation and masturbation desirable. When orgasm occurs, the brain releases chemicals like endorphins and oxytocin, causing euphoria, reducing pain, and potentially promoting attachment. These powerful chemicals often trigger the desire to repeat the act.

On the other hand, urination is a daily function, with the bladder filling with waste products over time. When the bladder is full, nerve endings signal the need to urinate. While a sense of relief can be experienced upon urination, especially if the bladder is very full, this is not the same as the pleasure of orgasm. It is more of an avoidance of discomfort.

However, there may be a small number of men who find urinating more satisfying than orgasm. See _URL_0_ for more information.";
